<p>
In the theme of NixOS enabling fearlessness, here&rsquo;s a way I use Nix on <i>other</i>
Linux distributions to sleep better at night: managing dependencies of simple
scripts. 
</p>



<p>
This way of using Nix is for everyone, not just those willing to try NixOS.
We&rsquo;ll look at it first for a Python script, and then see how it can be adapted
to any other language.
</p>



<p>
I wanted to create a script that would read the verbatim content of my articles
out loud to me with a synthesised voice. One of the more convenient ways to do this is with Python, where
I&rsquo;ve heard good things about Trafilatura for extracting the contents out of a
web page, filtering on types of content, etc.
</p>



<p>
But Trafilatura is a third-party Python dependency. Those suck because they
break your scripts when you so much as glance sideways at <code>pip</code>. The code also
ended up depending on <code>aplay</code>, which is a system dependency, making it two
different sources of future breakage. A couple of years ago, I would probably
have judged that to be too much maintenance, so I wouldn&rsquo;t have created the
script. Fortunately, now I know about Nix.
</p>


